{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.QBeta.C1ConnectionPoles","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.QBeta.C1ConnectionPoles","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic","lean:module:Mathlib.Topology.Algebra.InfiniteSum.Ring","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Pow.Real","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecificLimits.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Complex.Circle","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.Complex.LocallyUniformLimit","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.Complex.CauchyIntegral","lean:module:Mathlib.MeasureTheory.Integral.CircleIntegral","lean:module:QOU.Mathlib.CircleIntegralFinsetSum"],"declarations":36,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.argumentPrinciple_annulus_finset","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.argumentPrinciple_annulus_finset","kind":"theorem","line":1118,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/C1ConnectionPoles.lean#L1118-L1134","doc":"**The argument principle on an annulus, `Finset`-indexed** — the special case\nof `argumentPrinciple_annulus_filter` where every factor already lies strictly\nbetween the two contours, so the filter is the whole index set. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.argumentPrinciple_annulus_filter","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.argumentPrinciple_annulus_filter","kind":"theorem","line":1001,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/C1ConnectionPoles.lean#L1001-L1116","doc":"**The argument principle on an annulus, `Finset`-indexed**, with the factors\nallowed to sit anywhere off the two contours.\n\nFor `F = (∏ a ∈ S, (·−a)^{m a})·u` with `u` analytic and non-vanishing on a\nneighbourhood of the closed annulus, the winding difference counts *exactly the\nfactors between the two circles*, with multiplicity. A factor inside the inner\ncircle contributes `2πi` to both integrals and cancels; one outside the outer\ncircle contributes to neither.\n\nThe weaker hypothesis is what the θ instantiation needs. A factorisation\nobtained from `MeromorphicOn.extract_zeros_poles` is analytic on an *open* set,\nwhich must therefore be strictly larger than the closed contour annulus — so its\nzero set can spill into the two collars, and demanding `r < ‖a‖ < R` for every\n`a ∈ S` would be a hypothesis one cannot discharge without first proving the\ncollars zero-free. Counting with a filter removes the need entirely. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.logDeriv_prod_pow_sub_mul","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.logDeriv_prod_pow_sub_mul","kind":"theorem","line":978,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/C1ConnectionPoles.lean#L978-L998","doc":"Pointwise split of the logarithmic derivative of a finite product of powers\ntimes a non-vanishing factor. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.multiplicity_eq_one_of_winding_step","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.multiplicity_eq_one_of_winding_step","kind":"theorem","line":939,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/C1ConnectionPoles.lean#L939-L976","doc":"**The C1 payoff: any factorised zero in a fundamental annulus is SIMPLE.**\n\nCompose `qTheta_winding_step` (winding difference `= 2πi`) with\n`argumentPrinciple_annulus_single` (winding difference `= m·2πi`): the\nmultiplicity is forced to `1`.\n\nSo *conditional only on `θ_P` admitting a factorisation `(·−a)^m·u` on the\nannulus with `u` analytic and non-vanishing* — the classical finiteness fact,\nnot an extra assumption about `θ_P` — the zero in each fundamental annulus is\nsimple. Together with `qTheta_neg_zpow_eq_zero`, which puts a zero at `−p^k`,\nthat zero **is** `−p^k` and there is no other: the converse zero locus.\n\nThe remaining gap is therefore exactly the existence of the factorisation, and\nnothing else. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.argumentPrinciple_annulus_single","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.argumentPrinciple_annulus_single","kind":"theorem","line":850,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/C1ConnectionPoles.lean#L850-L937","doc":"**Argument principle on an annulus, for a single zero of known order.**\n\nIf `f = (· − a)^m · u` with `u` analytic and non-vanishing on an open `A`\ncontaining the closed annulus `r ≤ ‖z‖ ≤ R`, and the zero `a` lies strictly\ninside the annulus, then the winding difference is exactly `m`:\n\n    ∮_{|z|=R} f'/f  −  ∮_{|z|=r} f'/f  =  m · 2πi.\n\nThe `u'/u` part cancels by the annulus Cauchy theorem; the `m/(z−a)` part\ncontributes `2πi` on the outer circle (Cauchy) and `0` on the inner (holomorphic\nin the disc). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.logDeriv_pow_sub_mul","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.logDeriv_pow_sub_mul","kind":"theorem","line":835,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/C1ConnectionPoles.lean#L835-L848","doc":"`logDeriv` of `(· − a)^m · u` splits as `m/(z−a) + logDeriv u`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.integral_inv_sub_outside","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.integral_inv_sub_outside","kind":"theorem","line":819,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/C1ConnectionPoles.lean#L819-L833","doc":"`∮_{|z|=R} (z−a)⁻¹ dz = 0` when `a` is outside the circle. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.integral_inv_sub_inside","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.integral_inv_sub_inside","kind":"theorem","line":813,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/C1ConnectionPoles.lean#L813-L817","doc":"`∮_{|z|=R} (z−a)⁻¹ dz = 2πi` when `a` is inside the circle. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.qTheta_winding_step","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.qTheta_winding_step","kind":"theorem","line":747,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/C1ConnectionPoles.lean#L747-L798","doc":"**The winding number of `θ_P` rises by exactly one per period.**\n\n    ∮_{|z| = pρ} θ'/θ dz  =  2πi  +  ∮_{|z| = ρ} θ'/θ dz,     p = P².\n\nThe only hypothesis beyond `ρ > 0` is that `θ_P` has no zero on `|z| = ρ` —\nunavoidable, since otherwise the integrand is not defined there, and harmless,\nsince the radius is ours to choose.\n\nThe argument principle reads the left-hand side minus the right as *the number\nof zeros, with multiplicity, in the annulus `ρ < |z| < pρ`*, hence exactly one;\n`qTheta_neg_zpow_eq_zero` supplies that zero at `−p^k`, making it simple and the\nonly one, so the zero set is exactly `−p^ℤ` and the C1 pole divisor is exact\nrather than `⊇`. That is `QOU/QBeta/C1ThetaZeroLocus.lean`, which consumes this\ntheorem: `qThetaC_eq_zero_iff` and `qThetaC_analyticOrderAt_neg_zpow`.\n\nChecked numerically at our substrate in\n`computations/qbeta/c3_w2_c1_theta_zero_count.py`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.winding_step","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.winding_step","kind":"theorem","line":717,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/C1ConnectionPoles.lean#L717-L745","doc":"**A `1/w` in the integrand is a `2πi` in the integral.**\n\nAbstracted away from `θ_P`: any `g` that is circle-integrable on `|z| = R` and\nsatisfies `a·g(a z) = 1/z + g(z)` on that circle has\n`∮_{|z| = aR} g = 2πi + ∮_{|z| = R} g`. The `2πi` is\n`circleIntegral.integral_sub_center_inv`; the hypothesis `hstep` is exactly\nwhat the logarithmic-derivative functional equation supplies. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.circleIntegral_scale","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.circleIntegral_scale","kind":"theorem","line":704,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/C1ConnectionPoles.lean#L704-L715","doc":"**Scaling a circle contour.** `∮_{|z| = aR} f = ∮_{|z| = R} a·f(a·z)`.\n\nImmediate from the definition — `circleMap 0 (aR) θ = a · circleMap 0 R θ` —\nbut Mathlib has no change-of-variables for `circleIntegral`, so it is proved\nhere. Stated for its own sake: nothing about `θ_P` enters. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.logDeriv_qThetaC_functional_equation","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.logDeriv_qThetaC_functional_equation","kind":"theorem","line":676,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/C1ConnectionPoles.lean#L676-L696","doc":"**The `+1`.** The logarithmic derivative gains exactly `1/w` per period:\n\n    p·θ'(pw)/θ(pw)  =  1/w + θ'(w)/θ(w),   p = P².\n\nThis is the whole zero-counting mechanism in one line. Integrating over\n`|w| = ρ` and substituting `w ↦ pw` turns the `1/w` into `2πi`, so the winding\nnumber of `θ_p` on `|w| = pρ` is exactly **one more** than on `|w| = ρ`.\nThe argument principle reads that off as *exactly one zero, counted with\nmultiplicity, in every fundamental annulus*, which with\n`qTheta_neg_zpow_eq_zero` pins that zero at `−p^k` and makes it simple. That\ninference is taken in `QOU/QBeta/C1ThetaZeroLocus.lean`\n(`qThetaC_eq_zero_iff`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.deriv_qThetaC_functional_equation","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.deriv_qThetaC_functional_equation","kind":"theorem","line":642,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/C1ConnectionPoles.lean#L642-L674","doc":"**The functional equation, differentiated**: `p·θ'(pw) = θ(w) + w·θ'(w)`\n(with `p = P²`).\n\nLegitimate only because `differentiableOn_qThetaC` is available: the identity\n`θ_P(P²w) = w·θ_P(w)` holds on `ℂ*`, which is open, so the two sides agree on a\nneighbourhood of every `w ≠ 0` and their derivatives agree there. Without\nholomorphy there is nothing to differentiate. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.qThetaC_functional_equation","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.qThetaC_functional_equation","kind":"theorem","line":609,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/C1ConnectionPoles.lean#L609-L640","doc":"**The functional equation over `ℂ`**, `θ_P(P²w) = w·θ_P(w)`.\n\nSame proof as the real `qTheta_functional_equation`, re-run over `ℂ` because\neverything analytic downstream (`deriv`, contour integrals) needs the complex\nargument. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.differentiableOn_qThetaC","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.differentiableOn_qThetaC","kind":"theorem","line":552,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/C1ConnectionPoles.lean#L552-L600","doc":"**`θ_P` is holomorphic on `ℂ*`.**\n\nPartial sums are finite sums of `c·wⁿ`, differentiable off `0`; on any annulus\n`r < ‖w‖ < R` the Weierstrass M-test gives locally uniform convergence, and\n`TendstoLocallyUniformlyOn.differentiableOn` transfers differentiability to the\nlimit. This is what makes `θ_P` a genuine analytic object rather than a formal\n`tsum`, and it is the prerequisite for talking about its divisor at all. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.norm_le_annulus","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.norm_le_annulus","kind":"theorem","line":528,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/C1ConnectionPoles.lean#L528-L550","doc":"On `r ≤ ‖w‖ ≤ R` the terms are dominated by `qThetaTerm P R n + qThetaTerm P r n`,\nwhich is summable — the Weierstrass majorant. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.norm_qThetaCTerm","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.norm_qThetaCTerm","kind":"theorem","line":523,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/C1ConnectionPoles.lean#L523-L526","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.qThetaC_ofReal","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.qThetaC_ofReal","kind":"theorem","line":510,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/C1ConnectionPoles.lean#L510-L521","doc":"**The complex `θ_P` restricted to real arguments is the real one.**\n\nThe bridge every real fact about `θ_P` — the vanishing theorem\n`qTheta_neg_zpow_eq_zero` above all — crosses to reach the complex-analytic\nstatements. Unconditional: `Complex.ofReal_tsum` needs no summability, since\nboth sides are `0` when the family is not summable. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.qThetaC_eq_tsum","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.qThetaC_eq_tsum","kind":"theorem","line":508,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/C1ConnectionPoles.lean#L508-L508","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.qThetaCTerm","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.qThetaCTerm","kind":"def","line":504,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/C1ConnectionPoles.lean#L504-L506","doc":"One term of the complex-argument series. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.qTheta_inversion","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.qTheta_inversion","kind":"theorem","line":469,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/C1ConnectionPoles.lean#L469-L497","doc":"**Inversion formula** `θ_P(1/w) = w · θ_P(w)`.\n\nMorita (arXiv:1105.1998 §2.1) records it as `x·θ(1/x) = θ(x)` in his\nnormalisation; this is the same identity in ours. Reindex `n ↦ −n` on the left\nand `n ↦ n−1` on the right: both sides are `∑ P^{−n(n−1)} wⁿ`. Unconditional. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.connRatio_reflect","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.connRatio_reflect","kind":"theorem","line":456,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/C1ConnectionPoles.lean#L456-L467","doc":"**The reflection law behind `|χ| = 1` on the real axis.**\n\nWith `ᾱ = conj α`, the numerator and denominator of the closed form swap under\nconjugation: `N(z̄) = conj (D z)`. Hence `χ(z̄) = 1/conj χ(z)`, which forces\n`|χ| = 1` on `ℝ*` and pairs every zero with a conjugate pole — exactly the\nstructure measured (reflection law verified numerically to `1e-268`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.thetaRatio_multiplier","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.thetaRatio_multiplier","kind":"theorem","line":439,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/C1ConnectionPoles.lean#L439-L454","doc":"**The theta quotient `E_c(z) = θ_P(cz)/θ_P(z)` is a q-character with\nmultiplier `c`.**\n\nDivision-free form of `E_c(P²z) = c · E_c(z)`. This is the building block every\nconnection coefficient in the literature is made of: Lin–Ma–Xu\n(arXiv:2412.02281, Thm 1.2) writes them as\n`(constant) · θ_q(−aᵢz)/θ_q(−z) · z^{log_q aᵢ}`, and `θ_q(cz)/θ_q(z)` is the\nq-analogue of the power `z^{log_q c}`. Such coefficients are the\n*pseudo-constants* of Ramis–Sauloy–Zhang. It is also why the cross-ratio χ has\nconstant exactly `1`: the constants and the `θ(z)` denominators cancel. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.connRatio_elliptic","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.connRatio_elliptic","kind":"theorem","line":417,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/C1ConnectionPoles.lean#L417-L437","doc":"**The closed form is `p`-elliptic.**\n\nStated division-free as a cross-multiplied identity, so no hypothesis that the\ntheta values are nonzero is needed. The four functional-equation factors are\n`z/a`, `−z/b` upstairs and `−z/a`, `z/b` downstairs; their products are both\n`−z²/(ab)` and cancel. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.norm_qThetaC_conj_pair","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.norm_qThetaC_conj_pair","kind":"theorem","line":384,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/C1ConnectionPoles.lean#L384-L401","doc":"**The magnitudes at a conjugate pair of exponents agree EXACTLY.**\n\nThe `0`-side exponents are a conjugate pair `t`, `conj t`\n(`C1ZeroSideExponents.conjugate_pair_iff`). For **real** `z`,\n`z / conj t = conj (z / t)`, so by `qThetaC_conj` the two theta values are\nconjugates and their magnitudes are equal — hence so are `|e_t(z)|` for the two\nexponents, since they share the numerator `θ_p(z)`.\n\nA construction placing the two conjugate exponents \"~17 orders of magnitude\napart\" is therefore **impossible**, which is exactly what the numerics found\n(measured ratio `1.0`, deviation `0.0`) and what\n`computations/qbeta/c3_w2_c1_connection_matrix.py` records. No hypothesis on `t`\nis needed: at `t = 0` both sides are `θ_P(0)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.qThetaC_conj","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.qThetaC_conj","kind":"theorem","line":377,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/C1ConnectionPoles.lean#L377-L382","doc":"**`θ_P` has real coefficients, so it commutes with conjugation.** ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.qThetaC","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.qThetaC","kind":"def","line":373,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/C1ConnectionPoles.lean#L373-L375","doc":"The q-theta with a COMPLEX argument (the base `P` stays real). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.qTheta_div_eq_zero_on_orbit","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.qTheta_div_eq_zero_on_orbit","kind":"theorem","line":354,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/C1ConnectionPoles.lean#L354-L365","doc":"**The `∞`-side pole loci `z ∈ ∓T·p^ℤ`.**\n\nThe `∞`-side solutions carry `θ_p(z/T₊)` and `θ_p(−z/T)` in their denominators,\nso they blow up exactly where those vanish. For any `T ≠ 0` and any `k`, the\npoint `z = −T·P^{2k}` is such a zero. Instantiating `T := T₊` gives the `[−T]`\nlocus and `T := −T₊` the `[+T]` locus. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.qTheta_neg_one_eq_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.qTheta_neg_one_eq_zero","kind":"theorem","line":348,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/C1ConnectionPoles.lean#L348-L352","doc":"Specialisation `k = 0`: `θ_P(-1) = 0`. This is the `[−1]` pole — the one\ncoming from the `0`-side character's common numerator, hence a normalisation\nartifact rather than intrinsic connection data. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.qTheta_neg_zpow_eq_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.qTheta_neg_zpow_eq_zero","kind":"theorem","line":331,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/C1ConnectionPoles.lean#L331-L346","doc":"**The q-theta vanishes on the whole orbit `-P^{2k}`, `k ∈ ℤ`.**\n\nWith `p = P²` this says `θ_p(w) = 0` for every `w ∈ −p^ℤ`. No summability\nhypothesis: `Equiv.tsum_eq` and `tsum_neg` hold unconditionally, so the\ninvolution closes the goal outright. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.qTheta_functional_equation","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.qTheta_functional_equation","kind":"theorem","line":298,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/C1ConnectionPoles.lean#L298-L329","doc":"**The functional equation** `θ_P(P²w) = w · θ_P(w)`.\n\nWith `p = P²` this is the usual `θ_p(pw) = w·θ_p(w)` — what makes `θ_p(z/T)⁻¹`\nrealise the multiplier a slope-1 irregular singularity demands. Both sides equal\n`∑ P^{−n(n−1)} wⁿ`. Unconditional, like the vanishing theorem. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.summable_qThetaTerm","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.summable_qThetaTerm","kind":"theorem","line":238,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/C1ConnectionPoles.lean#L238-L296","doc":"**The q-theta series is summable** for `P > 1`, `w ≠ 0`.\n\n`|term n| = exp(−L n² + (M−L) n)` with `L = log P > 0`. Since\n`−L t² + B t ≤ B²/(4L)` for every real `t` (one completed square), the series is\ndominated by `C·e^{−|n|}` with `C = exp(B²/(4L))`, `B = |M−L| + 1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.qThetaTerm_pairing","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.qThetaTerm_pairing","kind":"theorem","line":195,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/C1ConnectionPoles.lean#L195-L208","doc":"**The heart.** At `w = -P^{2k}` the term at `2k−1−n` is the negative of the\nterm at `n`: the exponent is invariant and the sign flips. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.pairing","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.pairing","kind":"def","line":171,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/C1ConnectionPoles.lean#L171-L176","doc":"The pairing `n ↦ 2k−1−n`, an involution of `ℤ`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.qTheta","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.qTheta","kind":"def","line":168,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/C1ConnectionPoles.lean#L168-L169","doc":"`θ_P(w) = ∑_{n ∈ ℤ} P^{-n(n+1)} wⁿ`, the q-theta with `p = P²`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.qThetaTerm","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.C1Poles.qThetaTerm","kind":"def","line":165,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/C1ConnectionPoles.lean#L165-L166","doc":"One term of the q-theta series, `P^{-n(n+1)} wⁿ`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}